      "reviewer": "OpenAI Codex",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-08-25T11:54:07Z",
      "note": "The v1 anchor was transaction 0x3fe3a1883f0ae263a260f7d3e9b462468f4f83c2c88bb89d1dee5d7d24262b51, nonce 33, which yielded only 6.109951473560231892 WBNB to the beneficiary. Scanning the sender's onchain history and the pair's WBNB flows identifies 36 successful same-selector calls to helper 0x7d1e1901226e0ba389bfb1281ede859e6e48cc3d, nonces 5-41 except the unrelated nonce-11 native transfer. Transaction 0x5058c820fa0bb0daff2bd1b30151cf84c618dffe123546223b7089c8c2e18331 is the earliest successful defect execution at block 24912667, index 20; the final call is 0x885262f9bcccf8f80bb1a2173a5e3dd2f9c27a0dead0215139b983a7702ead34 at block 24913336. Exact receipt netting produces the recorded pair depletion. Proxy deployment, implementation deployment, Upgraded log 0, EIP-1967 slot transition, and stable runtime hashes establish the artifact and 6,134-second activation age. Cohort classification remains provisional: the privileged project address 0x6a963573b9a7aaef9cb9eee747e1b6b2c21b0520 deployed V4 implementation 0x79fe086a4c03c5e38ff8074dea9ee0a18dc1af4f at block 24910631 and activated it three blocks later; Hypernative's published timeline attributes this to a compromised key, while CertiK reports the team's statement that the vulnerability arose in its newly updated V4. If the privileged key was compromised and V4 was malicious attacker-installed machinery, the incident is excluded by METHODOLOGY.md rather than admitted as a code-age observation."
    }
